# Cursor pointer on clickable actions

Every interactive control that the user can click or activate must show `cursor-pointer` (or the design-system equivalent on a shared primitive).

## Apply to

- `<button>`, links styled as buttons, icon-only actions
- Chips, tabs, listbox options, toggle rows, and other custom click targets
- Elements with `onClick` / `role="button"` / keyboard activation that look clickable

## Do not rely on

- Browser defaults alone (`button { cursor: default }` in many resets)
- Hover color or underline without a pointer cursor

```tsx
// BAD
<button type="button" className="rounded border px-2 py-1" onClick={onSelect}>
  Retail
</button>

// GOOD
<button
  type="button"
  className="cursor-pointer rounded border px-2 py-1"
  onClick={onSelect}
>
  Retail
</button>
```

Prefer putting `cursor-pointer` on shared primitives (`Button`, `Chip` remove control, tab triggers) so call sites inherit it. Disabled controls should use `cursor-not-allowed` / `disabled:cursor-not-allowed`, not pointer.
